ROLE: evaluation_prosecutor  VOTE: yellow
MANDATORY_BASELINES: [{"name": "Standard serialize-then-call on a production serving engine", "why": "The L0 evaluation uses synthetic, normalized latency assumptions (t_args=1, assumed lognormal jitter). A fair baseline requires actual token-by-token decode timings vs real tool latency on a real inference engine to ground the `r` ratio.", "repo_or_cite": "vLLM or SGLang"}]
STRAWMAN_RISKS: ["Assuming arbitrary correctness rates (p_correct up to 0.99) are achievable independent of argument complexity", "Treating the 'wasted work' penalty (w) as a clean parameter instead of measuring real-world cancellation/rollback costs of side-effecting tools", "Collapsing the arg-decode distribution into a static average rather than capturing the burstiness of JSON decode"]
MISSING_METRICS: ["Empirical tool argument prediction correctness (p_correct) on real traces", "Empirical distribution of tool latency vs arg-decode latency (r) in real agent workloads", "Real-world cancellation latency and side-effect rollback costs (w)"]
KILLER_EXPERIMENT: Replay a real agent trace dataset (e.g., ToolBench or SWE-bench tool calls) to measure the true `p_correct` (match between speculated args and final committed args) as a function of the leading-arg fraction, mapped against empirical tool latencies. If no tools fall into the mathematically required `p_correct > p*` win region for their respective `r` ratios, the claim is falsified for practical workloads.
RATIONALE: The evidence rests entirely on an L0 analytic and Monte-Carlo latency model. While mechanically sound, the model exposes an extreme sensitivity to `p_correct` (requiring >0.92 for large tools). Without grounding `p_correct`, `r`, and `w` in empirical measurements from real agent traces, the evaluation fails to prove the strategy is viable in practice (EXP-0004).
